1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,247
Most people who need to change a project were never taught Git.

2
00:00:04,247 --> 00:00:12,748
Gangplank puts them on a branch of their own, in a browser, checks what they save, and hands the work to the team as a review request.

3
00:00:12,748 --> 00:00:14,652
The shared branch never moves.

4
00:00:14,652 --> 00:00:16,185
Gangplank runs on your PC.

5
00:00:16,185 --> 00:00:24,897
A PowerShell server listens on localhost, your browser is the interface, and nothing like git, Node or Python is needed.

6
00:00:25,597 --> 00:00:31,129
Inside the server: Smart Save, the Sentinel secret scan, and an audit log.

7
00:00:31,129 --> 00:00:33,849
You edit files in an ordinary folder.

8
00:00:34,549 --> 00:00:38,521
Out to GitHub or GitLab over HTTPS, on a branch of your own.

9
00:00:38,521 --> 00:00:41,250
AI help goes through Fairlead only.

10
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:46,123
Lazarette, the vault, is an opt-in source for Git tokens.

11
00:00:46,823 --> 00:00:54,796
Safe by design: your branch only, stop if it moved, secrets blocked, every write audited.

12
00:00:54,796 --> 00:00:58,195
Single sign-on and roles are planned, not built.

13
00:00:58,445 --> 00:01:00,470
Setup starts in Fairlead.

14
00:01:00,470 --> 00:01:04,798
One click installs a key for Gangplank in Windows Credential Manager.

15
00:01:04,798 --> 00:01:09,230
The key is never shown, and Gangplank reads it from there.

16
00:01:09,878 --> 00:01:10,678
Then launch.

17
00:01:10,468 --> 00:01:14,144
A local server starts and your browser gets a one-time link.

18
00:01:14,144 --> 00:01:19,236
This is the test-mode launcher, so the GitHub behind it is synthetic and Lazarette is off.

19
00:01:19,486 --> 00:01:25,137
The job: a watchstander must add weekend maintenance notes to the team project.

20
00:01:25,137 --> 00:01:31,507
Paste the address, connect, name a branch, and start.

21
00:01:32,207 --> 00:01:35,700
Gangplank downloads a fresh copy to a folder of their own.

22
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:44,608
They edit with ordinary tools: the notes, a readme line, and by mistake an old deploy script with a token in it.

23
00:01:45,308 --> 00:01:47,130
Smart Save lists what changed.

24
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:56,350
The script with the token is excluded automatically, and the Sentinel scan names it: a possible GitHub credential.

25
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:01,002
For the commit title, Refine using AI asks first.

26
00:02:01,002 --> 00:02:07,504
Only file names and change types go out, through Fairlead, to a local model.

27
00:02:08,204 --> 00:02:12,726
Publish asks again, then makes one commit on their branch.

28
00:02:12,726 --> 00:02:18,976
The review request asks again, then opens a pull request for the team.

29
00:02:19,726 --> 00:02:20,719
Now the trust check.

30
00:02:21,284 --> 00:02:23,230
A teammate pushes to the same branch.

31
00:02:23,930 --> 00:02:26,324
Gangplank sees it on refresh and stops: saving stays off until they get the latest.

32
00:02:27,024 --> 00:02:29,692
It never force-updates.

33
00:02:30,333 --> 00:02:32,866
The review request is listed for the team.

34
00:02:32,866 --> 00:02:37,983
Every write is in the audit log: the branch, the commit, the review, when and where, never contents or tokens.

35
00:02:38,683 --> 00:02:47,032
And the model call is on Fairlead's own log: client Gangplank, local route, served by a small local model.

36
00:02:47,653 --> 00:02:55,470
That was Gangplank in test mode: a synthetic GitHub, a live local model through Fairlead, and the real safeguards.

37
00:02:55,470 --> 00:02:57,596
Its own branch, its own review.

38
00:02:57,596 --> 00:02:58,396
Fair winds.
